#AUTO 104
#NEEDED 0
# eMMC scan-and-map (issue 110m) — read the WHOLE card and log where the
# NON-ZERO data is, without writing gigabytes to the SD.
#
# DE-SELECTED while the sparse dump (103) runs: that dump does the same
# zero-scan and logs its own MAP as it packs, so running this too would just
# read all 29 GiB a second time. Re-enable it on its own when you want a
# fast layout map without writing anything.
#
# For a mostly-zero card this is the cheap way to see the real layout: the
# map (a handful of LBA ranges) tells you where the GPT, boot chain, and
# partitions actually live. It is run-length encoding at the block level —
# the logged ranges are the data, the gaps between them are zeros.
#
# emmc_scan_map (in src/012-emmc.c) reads chunk by chunk via ADMA2 at
# HS200, checks each 64 KB chunk for any non-zero byte (early-exit), and
# logs coalesced "[scan] NON-ZERO start .. end" ranges, then a summary line
# with the range count and how many 64 KB chunks were non-zero out of the
# whole card. Reads the full ~29 GiB, so it takes a few minutes; the bottom
# LED fills as a progress bar and "[scan] at <lba>" prints ~every 256 MB.
#
# Read-only on the eMMC; the only SD writes are the debug log itself. The
# map then drives a sparse dump of just the interesting regions.

CALL emmc_init
CALL emmc_hs200
CALL emmc_scan
